      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
        LIC Jeevan Akshay VII (857) vs Jeevan Shanti (858): Single-Premium Annuity Choice — Income Now or Bigger Pension Later?
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        A retiree with a lump sum — provident fund, gratuity, a matured policy, a property sale — walks into an LIC branch with one question: "I have this money once; how do I turn it into monthly income for life?" LIC's two single-premium answers look almost identical on a brochure cover, and they are routinely confused with each other. The difference is a single word: <strong>immediate</strong> versus <strong>deferred</strong>. Jeevan Akshay VII (857) starts paying from the next instalment after you buy. New Jeevan Shanti (858) makes you wait 1–12 years you choose — and pays more per month for the wait. Everything else in this guide — the 10 options versus 2, the entry ages, the death benefits, the exit rules — flows from that one difference. Get both quotations for your age before deciding; the branch's written quotation is final for your case.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Quick Summary Box -->
    <div class="bg-brand-50 border border-brand-200 rounded-2xl p-6 space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold text-brand-900">Akshay vs Shanti in one view</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-brand-800 leading-relaxed">
        <li><strong>Jeevan Akshay VII (857) — immediate annuity:</strong> single premium, non-participating, non-linked. Pension starts from the next instalment (monthly, quarterly, half-yearly or yearly) after purchase. <strong>10 annuity options (A–J)</strong>, including life-only, guaranteed periods of 5/10/15/20 years, return of purchase price, 3% yearly increase, and joint-life variants.</li>
        <li><strong>New Jeevan Shanti (858) — deferred annuity:</strong> single premium, non-participating, non-linked. You choose a <strong>deferment of 1–12 years</strong>; pension starts only after it ends, but at a higher rate than an immediate annuity on the same money, because LIC held the lump sum in the meantime. Only <strong>2 options</strong>: deferred annuity for single life or joint life.</li>
        <li><strong>The deciding question:</strong> when do you need the first pension cheque? If the answer is "next month", Akshay is the route. If you have other income until a known retirement date, Shanti's deferment usually buys a materially bigger pension for the same lump sum.</li>
        <li><strong>Name trap, cleared:</strong> Jeevan Akshay <strong>VI is discontinued</strong>. The current plan is Jeevan Akshay <strong>VII, plan 857, UIN 512N337V07</strong>. If a brochure, agent note or old article says "VI", it is describing a plan you can no longer buy.</li>
        <li><strong>Both are irreversible:</strong> the annuity option you choose at purchase cannot be changed later, in either plan. The choice is made once, before signature — spend your comparison effort there.</li>
        <li><strong>Tax is the same for both:</strong> every rupee of annuity is taxable at your slab rate — no 10(10D) exemption on annuity income. The purchase price can qualify under section 80CCC within the ₹1.5 lakh 80C ceiling, old regime only (see our <a href="/guides/lic-pension-annuity-tax-tds-explained/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">pension tax guide</a>).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none text-sm leading-relaxed space-y-6 text-slate-700">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">1. The One Difference That Decides Everything</h2>
      <p>
        Both plans convert a lump sum into a guaranteed pension for life, at rates fixed when you buy — market movements afterwards do not change your pension. The difference is timing, and timing changes the maths completely.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <strong>Akshay VII</strong>, LIC starts paying almost immediately: choose monthly mode and the first pension arrives the next month. That immediacy costs you — the pension per rupee is lower than a deferred annuity's, because LIC starts paying out before it has earned anything on your money. With <strong>Shanti</strong>, you hand over the same lump sum and receive nothing for the deferment period — 1 year at minimum, 12 at maximum, subject to pension starting no later than age 80. In exchange, LIC credits guaranteed additions during the wait, and the pension that eventually starts is higher per rupee. Our Shanti guide quotes LIC's brochure illustration: ₹10 lakh at age 45 with the maximum 12-year deferment illustrates to roughly ₹99,400 a year (single life) starting at 57 — against roughly ₹62,300 a year for an immediate annuity on the same money in the comparison in our <a href="/guides/lic-pension-plans-compared-saral-shanti-umang/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">pension plans comparison</a>. That gap — about 60% more pension — is the price of patience, and it is the entire reason Shanti exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical rule follows directly: <strong>defer only across years you can fund from other income.</strong> If you retire at 60 and buy at 60, a 5-year Shanti deferment means five years with no pension from this money — you would need salary, another pension, or savings to bridge them. If you buy at 52 with a lump sum already in hand and a deferment ending at 60, the wait costs you nothing you were counting on, and the higher pension is nearly free money.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">2. Head-to-Head Table</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50 text-left">
              <th class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Feature</th>
              <th class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Jeevan Akshay VII (857)</th>
              <th class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">New Jeevan Shanti (858)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Annuity type</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Immediate — pension from the next instalment</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Deferred — pension after 1–12 years you choose</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
